  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 254 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\n>> HEAVY RAIN AND THUNDERSTORM POTENTIAL MONDAY MORNING\n\nThe active pattern continues into at least the first half of the \nwork week, starting with Monday. The trough developing over the \npacific northwest will begin to push eastward toward Iowa Sunday \nnight into Monday, briefly pushing upper level ridging through \nSunday afternoon. However, this ridging will be short-lived as \nreturn flow begins to advect warm, moist air up into the state \nbehind the 500 mb ridge and ahead of the trough. The warm air \nadvection wing will first arrive in Iowa on Monday morning, pushing \nnorth and eastward through mid-day. Some isolated pockets of heavy \nrainfall are possible with this early day activity, but should \nprogressive enough to negate flooding concerns. The severe risk also \nlooks fairly isolated, as instability will be meager (~500 J/kg) and \nelevated. However, a 45 to 55 kt 850 mb jet will accompany the theta-\ne push, bringing some gusty wind potential both with or without \nstorms. NBM winds are generally max out around 40 mph, but could \nvery well overachieve that and approach wind advisory criteria. \n\n\n>> SEVERE THUNDERSTORM AND FLASH FLOOD POTENTIAL MONDAY \nAFTERNOON\n\nBy Monday afternoon, better instability (~1500 J/kg) will begin to \ndevelop in the warm sector along with 50+ kts of deep layer shear. \nThe limiting factor will be warm low- to mid-level temperatures \nleading to a notable amount of convective inhibition. Therefore, any \nthunderstorms that occur will need to overcome this cap, which will \nbe most likely along the southwest to northeast oriented boundary \nfrom western into northern Iowa. This boundary is expected to be the \nfocal point for thunderstorm activity through the afternoon and \novernight into Tuesday. Monday afternoon, some strong to severe \nstorms will certainly be possible along the boundary, with hail and \nwind being the primary concerns. A tornado threat does exist as \nwell, thanks to a healthy amount of low level turning, but would be \ncontingent on storms becoming rooted at the surface. This \nconditional risk has prompted the storm prediction center to issue a \nDay 3 slight risk focused on western into central Iowa for Monday \nafternoon.  \n\nAlong this boundary will also be a quite moist environment with \nPWATs pushing 2\" or higher, which would put them at the high end of \nclimatological PWATs for this time of year, suggesting efficient \nrainfall production. Likewise, storm motions parallel to the \nboundary will lead to training storms and a heavy rain and flash \nflooding threat Monday afternoon through the overnight. The highest \namounts are currently being modeled over northern Iowa, with 24 hour \nQPF amounts of 3 to 5\" being depicted by 18z Tuesday. The location \nof course will be dependent on where the boundary sets up, so it's \nlikely this area will shift through the coming days. However, the \nsignal is certainly there for a heavy rain threat Monday night \nthrough Tuesday. Should these amounts be realized, flash flooding is \npossible if not likely, especially if it occurs in any urban areas. \nThe weather prediction center rightfully has slight risks for \nexcessive rainfall over Iowa every day starting tomorrow through \nWednesday.  \n\n\n>> FLOODING CONCERNS INTO THE MIDDLE OF NEXT WEEK\n\nThe aforementioned surface boundary continues to produce storms \nTuesday into Wednesday as well. Breaks will likely occur between \npockets of forcing, but heavy rain and storms persist each day \nthrough Wednesday and potentially beyond. The weather prediction \ncenter rightfully has slight risks for excessive rainfall over \nIowa every day starting tomorrow through Wednesday. In addition \nto the flash flooding concerns, we may see rivers beginning to \nrespond as well. GEFS-induced hydrologic ensemble forecasts \nsuggest many of the rivers over north central into southern and \neastern Iowa could be pushing into action or flood stage by the \nsecond half of next week. Unfortunately, the active pattern \nreally doesn't look to let up through the second half of the \nwork week and into next weekend, but solutions diverge at that \npoint.",
